lations are  clearly positive some trade promotes the  living standards of all participants by  allowing us to focus on those areas in  which we are relatively productive for  example during the 19th century a  substantial part of us productivity  growth was caused by the shift from  agriculture to industry today exports  are our new frontier  shifting resources into export  production will be one of the most  effective ways of increasing  productivity over the long run recent  trade developments as discussed earlier  have been excellent in order to  consolidate and extend these gangs  however it is more important than ever  for us to have a guiding principle to  justify and reevaluate our international  economic role role chapter seven of the  economic report suggests that we think  of a central goal of international  relations as the provision of  international public goods and the  mitigation of externalities concepts  that we have that have long been central  in thinking about the role of government  at the domestic level has long been  recognized that the market if left alone  will tend to under produce these Goods  just as government's need to provide  national defense protect the environment  and finance basic scientific research so  international arrangements are needed  for the provision of international  public goods chapter 7 discusses four  important categories of such goods  International Economic Cooperation peace  and order the environment and basic  research these are all areas in which  international cooperation can provide  benefits to the United States while also  benefiting other countries economic  cooperation may be the most fundamental  international public good of all  development and security are one example  spill overs from Economic Cooperation  all nations benefit as developing  countries grow in addition economic  development restraint may restrain  political pressures within countries  increasing international security in  conclusion let me note the several  unifying themes that run throughout this  year's economic report of the president  one is that most of the challenges we  face have deep roots in the past just as  the increase in inequality to name one  example has no single simple cause  neither do any of the challenges we face  of any single simple solution it is a  fan of fantasy to believe that any magic  policy wand can transport us back to the  income distribution or productivity  growth that American enjoyed in the  1960s it takes time to respond  effectively to  even more time to reverse trains that  have been two decades in the making to  take just one example among myriad the  purely economic benefits of hex tire  take 15 years or more to ripen the time  it takes for a hexed our child to grow  up and join the labor force the second  unifying theme is a message of hope  problems that seem intractable like  ballooning budget deficits can be  addressed but they are most effectively  addressed in ways that rise above  partisan partisanship too often I've  seen partisan battles who closed off  opportunities for major reforms that  would potentially benefit almost  everyone while hurting almost no one the  third unifying theme concerns the role  of government in dealing with these  long-term problems I believe that our  efforts to redefine the role of  government will be one of the lasting  achievements of this administration  something often called the New  Democratic vision our new vision focuses  focuses on opportunity and  responsibility at both the national  international and individual level the  government must assume the  responsibility of changing itself to  meet the enormous changes in our economy  that have already occurred and that will  continue to develop one of the first  steps is to demonstrate that we can be  fiscally responsible these fiscal  constraints mean that the government  cannot do everything instead of viewing  this as a problem I view it is it up as  an opportunity to redefine our mission  to focus on those tasks which are which  are uniquely the government's  responsibility and the most general  responsibility is to ensure that all  Americans have the opportunity to live  up to their potential individuals must  assume however the responsibility for  grabbing those opportunities many of the  long-term trends I discussed are  worrying instead of ignoring or  lamenting these challenges the nation  must embrace them transform transforming  these problems into opportunities we can  do this only if we set a coherent  economic agenda this administration has  already accomplished much with the  policies the last four years  over the next four years the  administration will continue to build on  those policies holding fast to its  vision of the government's role in  economy as the basis for an agenda to  promote growth opportunity and  responsibility thank you we answer some  questions thanks very much dr. Stiglitz  has to go testify before the Joint  Economic Committee but we have time I  think for a couple of questions um  here's one over here by the World Bank  the the international agenda that you  outline seems quite controversial in  Congress from many ends of the political  spectrum and I'm wondering how difficult  will your new job be in the absence of  support in Congress for the overseas  Private Investment Corporation and other  US government institutions dedicated to  promoting foreign investment by US  companies in emerging markets well the  issue of foreign investment is actually  one of the topics that we do discuss XM  lanes in chapter 7 there has been a  tremendous amount of foreign investment  one of the points that we emphasized in  the chapter is the important link  between this foreign investment on an US  export US trade this link means that  American companies investing abroad then  are in a position to to sing American  goods goods produced in America and sell  them abroad from our perspective then  that not only is it this goes back to  the theme that I talked about in my  remarks today international trade  international investment are win-win  situations we are better off as a result  of this the foreign country is better  off we are better off because we we  export more we are better off because we  get higher returns than we otherwise  would have gotten so as an economy we  are better off but for income foreign  countries are better off because of the  obvious growth that it that it brings to  them so  I view this as very much win-win  situations one of our job will be to try  to communicate that view and to say you  know this is not a Cold War issue this  is really a post Cold War II issue where  we have to look at how we can work  together to make us all better off  essentially argues that a little bit of  inflation is not necessarily a bad thing  in that actually trying to achieve zero  inflation would have harmful effects and  that contrary to the notion of the Fed  should get out ahead of the curve and  preempt inflation that they we should be  cautiously expansionary and wait until  we see inflation but what what does this  argument pretend for what does this  imply for where monetary policy should  be heading this year well a general  tenant of the administration not to  comment on Fed policy and and let me the  purpose of the report is to set the  stage for discussions which are going on  all the time about what are the stance  abroad or macroeconomic policy I think I  think macroeconomic policy is an issue  that is a concern to all Americans and  and ought to be a subject of public  policy to debate one just for those who  have had a chance to read the whole  report yet what we try to do in that  chapter is discuss a number of key  issues that have been on the public  policy debate one of them is what has  happened to narrow and how it the  evidence oh the the what has happened to  the level of unemployment below which  inflation starts to rise it used to be  thought that if unemployment got below  six percent or six point two percent  inflation ought to begin to rise we've  now had two more than two years in which  in unemployment is below six percent no  signs of rising inflation in fact the  core rate of inflation which is the rate  of inflation excluding the volatile  energy and  food prices has actually fallen during  the last year by about point three  percent now another aspect of of the  discussion the macroeconomic section is  that as our economy has changed as we've  moved into this low and stable  inflationary environment we need to  re-examine a whole set of issues  pertaining to macroeconomic policy one  thing that we we point out is that the  evidence the the data which is very  compelling that there are very high  costs of high inflation rates inflation  rates when they get about fifteen  percent do have very serious problems  for economic growth but in the kind of  world that we live in today we're in the  United States inflation has been low and  getting lower it has been very hard for  people to detect any significant  economic costs associated with this  inflation and then we cite one study  that shows that in fact if you try to  get inflation 20 you start running into  problems of adjustment down at the very  very bottom end the other point that we  we point out is that the evidence used  to be there used to be a lot of concern  that the price of dis inflation used to  be very high which meant that if you  overshot if you brought your  unemployment rate down to low and you  got a little bit of a bout of inflation  that inflation would start soaring there  would be people thought of it as a  precipice that you're on a precipice and  if you fell off that precipice things  got worse and worse and that it would be  very costly to undo the evidence  suggests that both of those propositions  there's very little support for them we  do knocks it on a precipice and the cost  of dis inflation are are relatively low  they're basically balanced with the cost  the benefits that you get when you when  you exceed the the target the narrow as  your target so that the risk picture the  risk analysis today with our low  inflationary environment low and stable  was considerably different than it did  save 15 years ago in a quite different  macro  nomics situation and as we go into this  new new situation we need to read the  debates many of these issues over again  you didn't discuss what some of us view  is a important underpinning of our  economy and that is our infrastructure  our ability to get raw materials to  manufacturing and get our goods and  services into the international market  without putting an additional burden on  the pricing structure from this country  can you make some comments about  infrastructure particularly how it's  going to be impacted by the tightening  budget situation that we're going to  face over the next five years there are  many aspects of America's infrastructure  and I think one has to keep in mind the  various aspects one of the aspects  that's very important in you know  America's infrastructure is our  telecommunication system and we do spend  quite a bit of time in the economic  report talking about our  telecommunication system and in  particular the reforms this was awesome  last year's economic report of the  president the reforms that were under in  the bill that was passed last year just  about this time I have and how these are  unfolding today our belief is very  strongly that by getting the right  regulatory structure that promotes  competition we can have a more vital  telecommunications infrastructure which  is really critical in in America's  dominance in international economic  policy the other part of the  infrastructure obviously are our robes  and highways and uh and water actually  there are there other pranks there's  always it and we took we will should i  say other that we have a very brief  discussion in the economic report of the  president in a section where we talk  about fiscal capital and the role of  physical capital I'm like so so  important it's not only the private  sector capital accumulation X important  but the infrastructure which is often  important complement the my remarked I  talked about how the government and the  private sector were complement error  complimentary this is one example good  structure can increase the effectiveness  and returns to private to private  capital there's a third aspect of our  infrastructure that I think is very  important which is our knowledge  infrastructure rrd our basic research  our university system which i think is  an essential part of our infrastructure  that really needs to can have continued  support in years at us ahead the free  trade area of the Americas is one of the  achievements how do you see this  developing and what do you think are the  biggest challenges to get to an  agreement in this in with these  countries well at the summit of the  Americas that occurred in Miami there  was an agreement among the countries of  Latin America other North America and  South American continents to form a  free-trade area over the coming decades  so there is a commitment the issue as  you as you ask is how do we go from  there from where we are here today to to  this free trade area nice i think it's  going to be a a challenge I think it is  is a something in which we will all gain  and I think it's particularly important  that North and South America not break  up into a set of competing smaller  regional groups but try to bring them  all together within a single free trade  area to do this we are going to need  fast track Authority and the president  has emphasized that a number of  occasions the importance of fast track  Authority I think everybody recognizes  that trade negotiations cannot really  proceed without fast-track Authority and  so I I do hope that we will be  successful in getting this fast track  Authority is is the major vehicle for  facilitating this progress  I would certainly agree that the that  exports are the new frontier and and I  and I laud the administration for its  objectives in that direction in that  context I wonder if you would care to  comment on the administration's budget  proposal which proposes to eliminate the  export source rule which the Treasury  Department found in 1993 to be a  stimulant in a good thing for exports  well I don't want to get into detail  coming on specific tax provisions in any  an assessment of any tax provision this  goes back to where you mark that I made  earlier my talk basically as we said the  private sector he has to be the source  of generating exports and jobs dealing  with the other major problems the real  question is the role government needs to  be focused on where it can be most  effective as a complement to the private  sector and it has to be careful that in  doing that it doesn't distort the  allocation of resources the real  question is it needs to create a level  playing field in order that that the  nation's resources be allocated to the  most efficient way not distorted one way  or the other the critical issue here is  yes it may promote exports but does it  do it at the expense of something else  and a full-employment economy it's  important for us to to change our frame  of mind as we've gone into a  full-employment economy it's important  to realize that when you push resources  in one way you're taking them away from  somewhere else if you have a level  playing field you you can be sure that  they're going to the area where the  returns are highest except in those well  define cases where there's a reason the  market is not getting the right signals  for instance example is we under invest  in in in basic research because people  aren't able to get the full returns we  under invest in and reducing pollution  because the polluters don't pay for the  full cost of their of their pollution so  there are some well-defined areas where  we we have to selectively intervene  because the market  not getting there the price system  doesn't convey the right information the  real question that one needs to ask in  the context of any of these tax  provisions is is there a compelling case  that the market by itself is having  insufficient incentives for promoting  exports and I think the judgment was  that in this particular case there was  not a compelling case I'm going to just  take privilege of asking a question up  here if picking up on what you were just  saying about a full-employment economy  as you look at the year ahead what can  we say about what's likely to happen to  average wages and if it's a positive  story can it be can it be a story that's  told without the fed feeling that it has  to trump on the brakes without Tony okay  well as as we said that the long-term  trend on on real wages has not has been  one has been the picture that we gave  you which is that that they have risen  but only at a very moderate rate or  perhaps even fallen the exact rate of  increased depends on how you adjust for  inflation if there is a bias in the CPI  of that it really changes the picture of  whether it's a slight decrease or a  slight increase but it's obviously you  can say it's not good enough now in the  last last year or two there are the  evidence is that real wages have been  increasing and if we use a corrected CPI  increasing even even more there are a  couple factors as we look forward that  may account for this rise if you look at  the relationship between real wages and  productivity real wages where you adjust  not by the CPI but by the GDP deflator  have been tracking productivity but  there's been a wedge over the last few  years between the CPI and the GDP  deflator this sounds very technical but  in fact that in other words the price  index that affects goods in general for  all goods and the price index that  effects consumption goods the good the  goods that workers consume have have  diverged a little bit we are forecasting  that this divergence will be reduced  markedly in the coming years and if that  happens and productivity stays at the  same rate real wages as defined by CPI  will start rising at a more rapid rate  moreover there are some evidence that  productivity may actually be rising  slightly the evidence is that as you  know one of the problems is that that  we've one of the issues that we  discussed an economic report of the  present in chapter 2 is that there are  significant measurement problems one  aspect of the measurement problem is  that there are two ways of measuring the  size of the economy one way focuses on  income the other way focuses on output  in all your economics courses you were  always taught that those two numbers are  supposedly the same but in fact when  these statisticians measure them they  come out different and they come out  very very different over the last  several quarters the difference has been  at the rate of one percent per year  actually the last the third quarter of  1996 the difference was two percent the  very big difference we think and reasons  given and the economic part of the  president that income provides the  better measure and that's the measure  that has traditionally been used for  measuring productivity if you look at  that measure productivity productivity  the rate of increase of productivity has  increased from it's one point one  percent trend that's prevailed since  1973 to about 1.4 1.5 percent so there's  an increase in productivity so if you  combine the increase in productivity  with a decrease in the wedge between the  GDP deflator and the CPI you get a  prospect for an increase in real wages  that is really quite significant over  the coming years so on that note we'll  be back here next year at this time  looking for good news thanks very much  doctor soon
 

